Subject: A compiled body can hold a global's value as a literal, and every write to it is seen

What this does
  - `rt->aot_gwatch` lists up to 16 global-object property atoms a compiled body holds as literals.
    A write to one moves `aot_pic_epoch`.
  - `qjs_aot_global_fold(ctx, atom, v)` is the body's entry check: true only if the property is a
    plain data property of the global object, shadowed by no lexical binding, holding exactly the
    int32 `v`. It adds the atom to the list, holding a reference released at runtime teardown, and
    invalidates the global cells once (which also moves the epoch).
  - The cells of a listed property are never marked directly writable, so the compiled direct
    store misses and takes the engine's store.

Why we need it
  A global read through a cell costs a generation compare and a slot load on every use even for a
  `var` that holds a constant. With the writes watched, a body can use the value as a literal and
  pay one test at its entry and the epoch test it already has after each safepoint.

Functions touched
  `js_aot_gwrite`, `js_aot_gwrite_slot`, `js_aot_gwatched` (new); `js_aot_content_store` (global
  objects), `JS_SetGlobalVarS` (both direct writes), `js_gv_cell_fill`, `qjs_aot_put_global`;
  `qjs_aot_global_fold` (new). Deleting a property, adding one, and changing its flags already move
  the epoch through `js_gv_cell_invalidate`. The slot-store audit `.local/specs/audit-slot-stores.sh`
  accepts `js_aot_gwrite` as a hook and passes.

No.

--- a/quickjs.c
